latest_plan = "Based on the reflection and the pattern we've identified, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Define the function `solve_6ea4a07e` that takes a `ColoredGrid` as input and returns a new `ColoredGrid`.\n\n2. Get the dimensions of the input grid using the `get_dimensions()` method.\n\n3. Find the non-zero color in the input grid:\n   - Iterate through the grid cells until you find a non-zero value.\n   - Store this value as `input_color`.\n\n4. Calculate the new color using the formula:\n   `new_color = (9 - input_color) % 5 + 1`\n   This ensures the new color is always between 1 and 5, and correctly maps the input colors to output colors as per the identified pattern.\n\n5. Create a new 2D list to hold the transformed grid:\n   - Iterate through each cell of the input grid.\n   - If the cell value matches the `input_color`, set the corresponding cell in the new grid to 0 (black).\n   - If the cell value is 0 (black), set the corresponding cell in the new grid to the `new_color`.\n\n6. Create and return a new `ColoredGrid` object with the transformed grid.\n\nHere's a Python code implementation of this plan:\n\n```python\nfrom rob_agi.colored_grid import ColoredGrid\n\ndef solve_6ea4a07e(input_grid: ColoredGrid) -> ColoredGrid:\n    # Get dimensions\n    rows, cols = input_grid.get_dimensions()\n    \n    # Find non-zero color\n    input_color = next(cell for row in input_grid.values for cell in row if cell != 0)\n    \n    # Calculate new color\n    new_color = (9 - input_color) % 5 + 1\n    \n    # Create new grid\n    new_grid = [\n        [0 if cell == input_color else new_color for cell in row]\n        for row in input_grid.values\n    ]\n    \n    # Return new ColoredGrid\n    return ColoredGrid(values=new_grid)\n```\n\nThis solution should work for all the provided examples and test cases. It correctly handles the color transformation and the inversion of the pattern from the input to the output, following the abstract rule we identified in our reflection."
